// TOOLCHAIN REPRO (not a yyjson feature) — an INDENTED `|` branch arm that
// follows a leading-`|>` CONTINUATION line is silently absorbed into the
// chain instead of being parsed as a branch, and every arm after it is
// reported as "stray continuation line". The first arm gets no diagnostic at
// all, which is the dangerous half: the arm does not fire, and nothing says
// so.
//
// Pure core-language finding, nothing yyjson-specific: reproduced here with
// std/io only. Surfaced while writing yyjson's write-side example, where a
// long build chain wants to wrap across lines.
//
// The rule, established by bisecting the four combinations:
//
//   head on ONE line          + INDENTED arms   -> OK   (read_config.kz)
//   head on ONE line          + column-0 arms   -> OK
//   leading-`|>` continuation + column-0 arms   -> OK   (build_request.kz)
//   leading-`|>` continuation + INDENTED arms   -> BROKEN, this file
//
// So the workaround is real and cheap — put the arms at column 0 — but it is
// undiscoverable from the diagnostic, which points at the SECOND arm and
// blames it for being stray.
//
// Separately (same bisect, different bug): a TRAILING-`|>` continuation
// (`foo() |>` at end of line, next call on the following line) is miscompiled
// even with no branches at all, emitting Zig that fails with
// `output_emitted.zig:N:M: error: expected ',' after field`. Leading-`|>` is
// the form that works; trailing-`|>` should be rejected at parse time rather
// than producing bad codegen.
//
// EXPECTED (if the association gap is fixed): compiles and runs, printing
//   a
//   read ok
//
// ACTUAL (current compiler): fails to build with
//   error[KORU010]: stray continuation line without Koru construct
//     --> ...:N:5
//      |
//    N |     | not-found |> std/io:print.ln("nf")
//      |     ^
//   (and the same for `| failed`; the `| ok` arm above them is swallowed
//   without any diagnostic)
//
// Flipping the three arms below to column 0 — changing nothing else — makes
// this file compile and run.

~import std/io

~std/io:print.ln("a")
|> std/io:read-file(path: "/etc/hosts")
    | ok t |> std/io:print.ln("read ok") |> std/io:free(content: t)
    | not-found |> std/io:print.ln("nf")
    | failed m |> std/io:print.ln("{{ m:s }}")
